A||N||10||GT-maniac||Exhaust manifold replacement||||9||
Z||000000||GT-maniac||09-07-2001||12:46 PM||||I have a couple of questions, first my front (firewall side) exhaust manifold on my 87 GT is cracked. From all I've read on here this looks like it will be a PITA to fix. Especially since the car has air conditioning. Now since the car has apparently never had any recall work done on it, should I get the front manifold replaced by the dealer since it apparently was part of one of the recalls ? I'm a little leary about taking the car to the dealer as I'm worried about them screwing something else up. Also are both manifolds the same ? I have a cracked rear manifold that I could get welded if I need to.  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero005a.jpg"> <P>85 WS6 GT partly modified<BR>87 GT Loaded resto project||||reg||9
Z||000001||FieroBUZZ||09-07-2001||12:54 PM||||Try a search for recalls.  If no work has been done to your car, Pontiac should pony up and do the manifold etc. for you.  The dealer's work should be guaranteed, so if they screw it up, they fix it again.  You may have to fight a bit to get it but go for it.<P>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||09-07-2001||01:17 PM||||I don't think cracked manifolds were covered under the recall.  The recall was for the rubber gasket in the rear of the deck lid, It gets tore off, and a heat shield back there some where.<P>Replacement exhaust manifolds (NOS) are pricey and you are correct a PITA to replace. I had the dealer replace the front, a piece of cake because the engine was out, but a year later the exhaust manifold gasket blew and that was a labor intense ($$) to fix. Past the years warrenty. They gave me a break because I'd spent a fortune at the shop getting the car painted and repaired.<P>The rear crack got welded.<P>I should have gotten the hedders.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steve Normington||09-07-2001||02:19 PM||||The front and rear manifolds are different. One way to make the repair easier is to drop the rear end of the cradle.<P>Remove the decklid.<BR>Jack the car up from the rear as far as it will go (make sure you have good chocks under the front wheels.<BR>Place a jackstand under each of the front cradle mount brackets. The jackstands go on the car side, not the cradle side. IE, there are two metal brackets that are welded to the rear body of the passanger compartment that are on the outside of the cradle bushings. I generally use the inner bracket.<BR>Undo any exhaust hangers that attach to the body (you shouldn't have to undo the springs).<BR>Remove the two rear cradle bolts (you may need an impact wrench for these).<BR>While you watch from the top of the engine compartment, have an assistant <B>SLOWLY</B> lower the jack. You'll want to watch the wiring harness and the clearance between the engine and the trunk.<BR>You'll need a really sturdy and stable step stool because the car will be about 18" higher that before.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||09-07-2001||07:07 PM||||Steve,<P>Sounds like the voice of experience.<BR>I'm about to have to tackle this. Once you have it all jacked up and the cradle dropped, do you work from above? Or from underneath? Or both. <BR>I'm <I>not</I> looking forward to this.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||09-08-2001||09:07 AM||||I did my front manifold the same way steve did.  I undid the dogbone, too, to get the rear of the cradle down more.  Get lots of penetrating fluid on the bolts before you try to pull them.  <P>I used new stainless steel capscrews and flat/lock washers from Lowe's, but some people use studs from a Saturn engine.  Ed Parks was helpful with part numbers for those, but I still chose the stainless route.<P>I'd really try the recall route first, so you can get the front exhaust manifold heat shields.  In case you get the shields from a junkyard, I modified the lower (flat) shield so it would fit on the outside of the manifold flanges, and it hasn't leaked yet.  This worked better than placing it against the exhaust ports, since I used different hardware than GM did.||||reg||1
Z||000006||skitime||09-08-2001||11:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>I don't think cracked manifolds were covered under the recall.  The recall was for the rubber gasket in the rear of the deck lid, It gets tore off, and a heat shield back there some where.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Old Lar the somewhere is under the manifold which has to come off to put it here.  The recall is for all oil leaks and exhaust leaks including the manifolds.<BR><P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mickey_Moose||09-08-2001||11:41 AM||||I have a 88 GT that I fixed the manifolds on this spring - and have to say that it was not that hard to do (considering I also had a broken bolt on the front side)<P>remove deck lid, disconnect battery, loosen off the air compressor and let it rest on the cradle (jack and block the car so you can get under it), and that is all. Actually I would also suggest to disconnect the EGR pipe from the ERG valve as you don't want to break this when you are moving the exhaust around.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>rear side easy to get at.<P>front side top 3 bolts undo from the top, bottom 3 from underneath the car.<P>bolts seemed to be a mild steel so be care not to break, soak overnight in some good penatrate oil, give another shot in the morning and go to work in the afternoon. Work them back and forth till they loosen up and screw them out.<P>recall &gt;&gt; involved adding heat shields around the manifold, which you have to remove before getting at the manifolds. They are mounted to the manifolds via a stud that also mounts the manifold to the head.<P>Bolts are a M8 with a 1.25 thread pitch. The studs have to be made - there is no replacement.<P>Once you got it apart, should think are porting them, and beefing up the welds on the flanges as well  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Getting them off took me a good afternoon, due to the learning curve - next to impossible to remove the bottom front manifold bolts from the top (which I wasted a lot of time on) - really easy from under the car. Had it all back together and running, once everything was fixed, in about 1.5 hour||||reg||6
Z||000008||Steve Normington||09-08-2001||04:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>I'm about to have to tackle this. Once you have it all jacked up and the cradle dropped, do you work from above? Or from underneath? Or both. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>From above.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2birds:<BR><B>I did my front manifold the same way steve did.  I undid the dogbone, too, to get the rear of the cradle down more.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Thank you, I forgot that part. You might also have to remove the distributor cooling tube.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||jelly2m8||09-08-2001||05:22 PM||||Use Steve's method, It gives you the most work room without dropping the cradle entirely.<P> After you have done a few hundred of these, you will be able to do them with just removing the decklid.........||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||09-08-2001||07:23 PM||||Good info. Thanks guys!<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||14
